2 | general | Henry Spangler farm: lies between the Emmitsburg Road (east) and Seminary Ridge (west); the Codori farm bounds it on the north and the Sherfy farm on the south (as bounded and owned in 1956) | - | ~1956 (boundaries and owners as of the statement; the road and the ridge are fixed features) | stated | The statement says the Henry Spangler farm lies between the Emmitsburg Road on the east and Seminary Ridge on the west, bounded on the north mainly by the Codori farm, then owned by the United States Government, and on the south by the Sherfy farm, then owned by the Smyser Folkenroth estate | =The Henry {{Spengler farnlies betiveen|Spangler farm lies between}} the Emmitsburg {{Road.(on|Road (on}} the {{éust)|east)}} and Seminary Ridge on the west. It is bounded on the north mainly, by {{thé|the}} Codori {{farms|farm,}} owned by the United States Government, and on the south by the Sherfy {{fara, ‘owed:by thé|farm, owned by the}} Smyser Folkenroth estate. ~O
2 | fields | Henry Spangler farm: 130.84 acres when the Government bought it on March 28, 1955 (typed 130;84 in the typescript, read as the decimal point) | - | ~1955 (the acreage of the farm at its purchase; the statement gives no 1863 acreage) | stated | The statement gives the farm, as the United States Government bought it on March 28, 1955, as 130.84 acres | =The farm, consisting of {{130;8) acre’,|130.84 acres,}} was {{purciiaged|purchased}} by the United States Government March 28, 1955. ~O
2 | woodlots | Spangler's Woods on Seminary Ridge: a 25-acre parcel that was part of the original Spangler farm until the Government bought it in 1899 | - | ~pre-1899 (part of the original Spangler farm until 1899; the statement gives no 1863 date) | stated | The statement says Spangler's Woods on Seminary Ridge, a 25-acre parcel that was part of the original Spangler farm, was purchased by the Government in 1899 | ={{Spangter!s|Spangler's}} Woods on {{Semindry Ridge,|Seminary Ridge, a}} 25-acre parcel {{which.was|which was}} part of the original {{Spanglér farms|Spangler farm,}} was {{urchased bythe Governnent|purchased by the Government}} in 1899. ~O
2 | general | Henry Spangler farm in the battle: Pickett's division formed battle lines and awaited orders to attack in the low ground in the vicinity of the Spangler buildings | - | 1863-07 (July 3 by the statement's own page 4) | stated | The statement says the entire farm is closely associated with Pickett's Charge because the division formed battle lines and awaited orders to attack in the low ground in the vicinity of the Spangler buildings | =The entire farm {{ia|is}} closely associated with Pickett's Charge, {{asa contimious|as a continuous}} line of {{‘artilidry|artillery}} extended from south to north over {{this-land|this land}} and {{Pickbtt's|Pickett's}} entire {{dividion|division}} of {{1 ;80d mon fornedbattle|4,800 men formed battle}} lines and {{avalted|awaited}} orders to attack in {{tha lew|the low}} ground in the vicinity of the Spangler buildings. ~O
4 | general | Henry Spangler farm: on July 3 the low ground at the farm buildings was screened from the Union positions on Cemetery Ridge by the Emmitsburg Road ridge | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The statement says that, arriving late in the day at Spangler's Woods, the division moved forward near midday on July 3 to the low ground at the Spangler farm buildings, where its position was screened from the view of Union positions on Cemetery Ridge by the Emmitsburg Road ridge | =Arriving {{Jato‘|late}} in the day at Spangler's {{Woodsy,|Woods,}} the division moved forward near midday {{dply|July}} 3, to the low ground at the Spangler farm buildings where their position was {{séreened|screened}} from the {{vir.|view}} of {{Uniori ‘positioris|Union positions}} on Cemetery Ridge by the {{Iraltsburg|Emmitsburg}} Road ridge. ~O
